{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba packages/lhc/docs/02-specs/05-derivation-inference/tech-design.md | sed -n '175,205p'\"","aggregated_output":"   175\t### Flow 5: Turn-End Boundary Advance (AC-5.1–5.5)\n   176\t\n   177\tTwo modules change. In `pipeline.ts`, the walk already knows whether the batch contained a `turn_end` (it processes the event kinds); the advance registration at line ~150 gains that condition. Everything about the registration — first-before-poke order, throw isolation, both host modes, flush-after-COMMIT — is untouched.\n   178\t\n   179\tIn `boundary.ts`, the zone read gains `turn_id` and the decision regroups:\n   180\t\n   181\t```\n   182\treadZoneToolResults: + turn_id per row (same WHERE, same order)\n   183\tgroupZone: consecutive rows by turn_id → groups, each { turnId, sourceEventOrders, tokenSum }\n   184\t           (turn_id NULL ⇒ singleton group, DD-10)\n   185\tadvanceDecision(groups, { maxTokens, targetTokens }):\n   186\t  total ≤ max → null\n   187\t  walk groups oldest-first, excluding the newest group always:\n   188\t    evict group only if (remaining − group.tokenSum) ≥ target   ← peek-ahead, AC-5.3\n   189\t    newPosition = group's highest sourceEventOrder\n   190\t  return newPosition (lands in [target, target + one group))\n   191\t```\n   192\t\n   193\tWorked example (golden G2 in the test plan): groups of 30k/25k/20k/15k tokens (oldest→newest), max 64k, target 32k. Total 90k > max. Evict 30k → 60k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Evict 25k → 35k remaining, ≥ 32k ✓. Peek 20k: 35k − 20k = 15k < 32k → stop. Boundary lands after the 25k group; zone = 35k, in [32k, 32k+20k). The 15k newest group was never a candidate.\n   194\t\n   195\tThe shared-query invariant survives: `visibilityZoneTokens` (status's sum) and the decision's walk read the same WHERE-clause population, so status and advance can never disagree about the zone — the property the existing code makes structural stays structural.\n   196\t\n   197\t`VisibilityBudgets` loses `floorTokens` everywhere it appears: the type, `resolveViewConfig`'s defaults and validation (which gains `maxTokens > targetTokens`), and the status read's reporting shape is unchanged (`zoneTokens`/`maxTokens` already). Existing Epic 03 tests asserting floor behavior are superseded — the test plan's amendment ledger names each one (the spec-sanctioned test-change path, as Epic 02's F-3 established).\n   198\t\n   199\t### Flow 6: CLI Retirement (AC-6.1–6.3)\n   200\t\n   201\tDeletion plus proof. Delete `src/cli/`, the `src/cli.ts` bin entrypoint (a separate file outside the directory — it imports `./cli/index.js` and breaks the build if left), the `bin` manifest entry, and the `dev:cli` script; delete `src/providers/registry.ts` and its two `sdk.ts` re-exports; delete the twelve `cli-process-*.test.ts` files; rewrite `verify`/`verify-all` (the `SKIP: cli-process` echo and `LHC_PROCESS_SUITE` gate go; `verify-all` becomes red-verify + full vitest including the self-accounting inference suite). The proof is three-legged: a public-API surface snapshot test (import the package entry, assert the export-name set against a checked-in expected list — catches both leftover CLI exports and accidental removals), a source-level grep test for `LHC_PROVIDER`/registry references, and the unchanged-SDK-coverage check (the full remaining suite green proves no behavior test rode out with the process suites — process suites asserted *parity*, never unique behavior, which is why deletion is safe).\n   202\t\n   203\tThe Epic 04 deviation note is written into this pack's deviation table at story completion: spawned `inspect health` parity was backfilled before Epic 05, and this story deletes the spawned-process parity surface instead of carrying it forward.\n   204\t\n   205\t## Interface Definitions\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
